md`## 7. Candidates for the Brain's backlog

Flagged only. Tom decides.

1. **Look for a closed issue whose tests passed in a harness that is not cb4 and whose change did not work there.** Reason: section 1. One found would say what the check on cb4 has to cover.
2. **Measure a brief the overseer edited against the one before it.** Reason: section 2. The tracker has rounds sent back per issue; the same count before and after an edit is the cheapest form.
3. **Price a cluster browser a month and set it beside $0.37.** Reason: section 3. Agent37's own limit (no Docker inside) is the thing to check against what the Brain's containers run.

The candidates of 9, 11 and 12 October: nothing recorded here says any was acted on.`
// %%
md`## What was not read

- arXiv: the index has it at 0 items this run, marked \`lookup\`. Hugging Face matched 0 of 50 again, so \`papers.json\` has three papers, all by mention; the third, Byte Language Models, was in the digest of 11 October.
- Reddit: nothing, the feed answered 429.
- The papers themselves. Both arXiv items are known by their abstracts. OpenAI's FFT preprint, Rich Hickey's article and every source Import AI links were not opened.
- Comments, on every item.
- Figures and scripts: the benchmark tables of Muse Wonder, the formulas of Recht's post, the charts of Auto Research.
- Parts of pages: two thirds of the StudentSim post, the READMEs of SolidCraft and Blokkily past their first screen, Kevin Kelly's page past its opening, the Tom's Hardware article (fetched, not read).
- z.ai returned a script and no text; patreon.com answered 403.
- 67 of the 103 items loaded below were in the files of 11 or 12 October. The 30 of Hacker News, one of Lobsters, Import AI 476, one post of Microsoft Research and three of Simon Willison are new.
- No item in this run from Karpathy's two feeds, Lilian Weng, Sebastian Raschka, Eugene Yan, Chip Huyen, Hamel Husain, BAIR or Meta engineering. Meta's release came by Hacker News.`
